consiSt("mt",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Montana Supreme Court","d":"The only appellate court for district court cases: Montana has no intermediate court of appeals, so every civil appeal goes straight to the Supreme Court, which also hears original proceedings and writs of supervisory control and answers certified questions. A chief justice and six associate justices, elected statewide for 8-year terms. The Court adopts the rules of procedure and governs admission, pro hac vice, and lawyer discipline."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts (22 judicial districts)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized into 22 judicial districts, from one judge in many rural districts to 10 judges in the 13th District (Yellowstone County). The Montana Rules of Civil Procedure govern all civil actions and proceedings here, including probate unless the Uniform Probate Code says otherwise."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Justices’ Courts, City Courts, and Municipal Courts","d":"Courts of limited jurisdiction listed in Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-101. They use separate rules (the Justice and City Court Rules of Civil Procedure and Uniform Rules), and appeals from them go first to the district court."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Water Court and Workers’ Compensation Court","d":"Statewide specialized courts. Water Court judges and the workers’ compensation judge are outside the peremptory substitution statute, and all Workers’ Compensation Court appeals go to the Supreme Court and are subject to mandatory appellate mediation."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2025)","d":"Montana enacted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act in 2025 as Title 27, chapter 34. It is a true anti-SLAPP statute with a special motion, an automatic stay, mandatory fees for a winning movant, and an immediate appeal. The MCA pages collected do not show the effective date or whether it reaches cases already pending; check Chapter 250, Laws of 2025, before relying on it in a case filed in 2025.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-101 to -112 (Ch. 250, L. 2025)","v":true},{"t":"Scope: three kinds of protected activity","d":"Covers any claim in a civil action against a person based on (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of freedom of speech or press, assembly, petition, or association under the U.S. or Montana Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nExcluded: claims against a governmental unit or its employees or agents acting officially; government enforcement actions against an imminent threat to public health or safety; and claims against a seller or lessor of goods or services arising from a communication about its own goods or services. Creating or promoting dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, or artistic works is not “goods or services.” The Act must be broadly construed to protect these rights.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-102, -111","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 60 days to file, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","d":"File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the complaint, counterclaim, crossclaim, third-party complaint, or other pleading, or later for good cause. Filing stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending motions, until the ruling and the time to appeal run out; on motion the court may also stay related matters with other parties. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific information is needed to meet a burden and is not otherwise reasonably available.\nThe court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing (later for allowed discovery or good cause) and rule within 60 days after the hearing. Not stayed: fee motions, voluntary dismissal or severance, and, for good cause, unrelated motions and public-safety injunctions. Montana added an exception: the stay does not apply to actions for orders of protection under Title 40, chapter 15.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-103, -104, -105, -108","v":true},{"t":"Standard: dismissal with prejudice","d":"The court looks at the pleadings, the motion papers, and any evidence usable on summary judgment under M. R. Civ. P. 56. It must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the Act applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to make a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the movant’s right to a ruling and fees; a voluntary dismissal with prejudice makes the movant the prevailing party.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-106, -107","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","d":"The court must award costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a movant who prevails. A responding party gets them only if it prevails and the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-110","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: of right, on a Montana-specific clock","d":"A movant may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or part. The statute sets its own deadline: 30 days after entry of the order between private parties, 60 days if the United States, the State, or a political subdivision is a party. An appeal stays all proceedings between all parties until it ends (subject to the same exceptions).","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-109, -104(3)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: written application through the Bar, leave of the presiding court","d":"A lawyer admitted in the highest court of another state may appear in a Montana action with leave of the presiding court if an active Montana lawyer is associated as attorney of record. Get the application from the Bar Admissions Administrator (State Bar of Montana) and file it there with a fee equal to the annual dues of an active Montana member (no dollar amount is stated in the rule). The Administrator certifies your prior appearances, good standing, and local counsel’s standing, then sends it to the court or agency, which grants or refuses by order and must state reasons for a refusal.\nThe form asks for every court you are admitted in, discipline and pending complaints (with copies), your firm’s pending Montana pro hac vice matters, and every Montana application you have made.","c":"Mont. Bar Admission R. VI.A, VI.D–G","v":true},{"t":"Two-appearance limit, residency bar, and continuing dues","d":"After a lawyer’s or firm’s second pro hac vice appearance (counting appearances after November 17, 1998), no more are allowed except on good cause, which is “not to be routinely granted” (examples: expertise not commonly available in Montana, or multistate class actions). You cannot appear pro hac vice at all if you live in Montana, are regularly employed there, or practice or do substantial business there, unless your bar application is pending. You must keep paying annual dues and fees each year the case is pending and tell the Administrator when the matter ends. A pro bono lawyer may pay a one-time fee instead.\nExceptions: no local counsel or fee for ICWA/Montana ICWA child custody appearances for a tribe, parent, or Indian custodian, or for federal government lawyers in the Water Court.","c":"Mont. Bar Admission R. I.G.6, VI.B, VI.C, VI.H, VI.K–M","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must actually work the case","d":"The Montana attorney of record must sign all pleadings, motions, and briefs and participate actively in all phases, including attending depositions and hearings and preparing discovery responses and briefs. The court or agency may waive this only on local counsel’s motion showing extraordinary circumstances, and waivers are “not to be routinely granted.”","c":"Mont. Bar Admission R. VI.J","v":true},{"t":"Sworn certification to the Supreme Court before you practice","d":"A lawyer who is not an active Montana Bar member and seeks to practice in any state or federal court in Montana pro hac vice must, before practicing, certify in writing and under oath to the Montana Supreme Court that the lawyer will be bound by the Montana Rules of Professional Conduct (except Rules 6.1–6.4) and subject to Montana discipline, and mail a copy to the State Bar in Helena. This is separate from the pro hac vice application.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 8.5(b)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission","d":"Montana adopted the ABA multijurisdictional-practice safe harbors: temporary services with an actively participating Montana lawyer, services related to a proceeding in which you are or expect to be authorized to appear, ADR-related work, and work arising out of your home-state practice. In-house counsel (including qualifying foreign in-house lawyers) may work from a Montana office for their employer, but not in matters requiring pro hac vice.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are built into the civil rules","d":"A lawyer may provide limited representation under Rule 1.2(c). Ghostwriting is allowed: you may draft a filing for a self-represented person without signing it, and may rely on the client’s facts unless you have reason to believe they are false. Appearing in court is a general appearance unless you file and serve a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding; service on you is then valid only for that proceeding, and you are out on filing a notice of completion, without leave of court.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 4.1, 4.2, 11(e); Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(c)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: pro hac vice lawyers may register","d":"Montana’s statewide e-filing system is open to licensed Montana attorneys and staff, attorneys appearing pro hac vice, and others the court allows. Registering is consent to electronic notification of service and to e-file everything in the case the system accepts. Updating your e-filing profile does not update the courts’ contact records; notify each clerk separately. Participation is voluntary unless a court designates a case type for mandatory e-filing.","c":"Temp. E-Filing R. 2(b), 3(a), 3(c), 6(b)(2) (AF 14-0745, as amended Apr. 24, 2024)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Montana Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Adopted by the Montana Supreme Court; the State Law Library compilation shows the last amendment on October 29, 2020 (AF 09-0688). Based on the ABA Model Rules but with Montana text and numbering (no official comments are printed in the compilation).","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct (AF 09-0688, through Oct. 29, 2020)","v":true},{"t":"Different numbering after Rule 1.16","d":"Rule 1.17 is a Montana rule on full-time government lawyers taking outside work; Rule 1.18 is the mandatory IOLTA program; sale of a practice is Rule 1.19 (entire practice; client consent presumed after 90 days’ notice); duties to prospective clients are Rule 1.20. Rule 7.4 has been eliminated. Cite carefully.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.17–1.20, 7.4","v":true},{"t":"Opponents cannot use the Rules as weapons","d":"Montana’s preamble says a violation does not give rise to a cause of action or a presumption of breach, does not necessarily warrant disqualification, and that an opposing party or lawyer has no standing to enforce the Rules in a collateral proceeding outside the disciplinary process. Expect this language in response to motions to disqualify.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct, Preamble ¶ (21)","v":true},{"t":"Fee terms in writing, with a $500 floor","d":"Scope of the representation, any change in scope, and the basis or rate of fees and expenses must be communicated in writing, before or within a reasonable time after starting. Exceptions: a regularly represented client billed on the same basis, and matters reasonably expected to cost the client $500 or less. Contingent-fee agreements must be signed and must say whether expenses come off before or after the fee.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Liens are restricted","d":"A charging lien may be asserted only against causes of action or counterclaims in litigation, as and to the extent Mont. Code Ann. 37-61-420(2) allows. A retaining lien may reach only the client’s papers and property for the unpaid matter, a written lien agreement must spell out those limits, and on termination you must hand over whatever the client reasonably needs for that matter. You bear copying costs for file copies the client requests.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(a)(2), 1.8(i), 1.16(d)","v":true},{"t":"Living-expense loan guarantees allowed","d":"Beyond advancing costs, a lawyer may guarantee a loan from a regulated lender solely for a client’s basic living expenses if needed to withstand litigation delay, the client stays ultimately liable, and the help is not offered or advertised before retention.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Trust records for five years; unclaimed funds to the MJF","d":"Trust records must be kept five years after the representation ends. Funds whose owner cannot be found for at least two years may be paid to the Montana Justice Foundation, which refunds them if the owner turns up within two years. IOLTA participation is mandatory.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 1.15(a), (f), 1.18(b)","v":true},{"t":"No ABA Rule 8.4(g)","d":"Montana’s misconduct rule lists only paragraphs (a) through (f); there is no harassment-or-discrimination paragraph like ABA Model Rule 8.4(g).","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4","v":true},{"t":"Cannabis advice expressly permitted","d":"The preamble states a lawyer may counsel and assist a client on Montana’s cannabis laws, but where Montana law conflicts with federal or tribal law, the lawyer must also advise on the related federal and tribal law and policy.","c":"Mont. R. Prof. Conduct, Preamble ¶ (6)","v":true}],"style":"State of Montana","home":"https://courts.mt.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Substitution of judge: 30 days and the fee, or it is void","d":"A peremptory substitution must be filed within 30 calendar days of service (or of the first summons served or an adverse appearance, for the plaintiff), and in a civil case it is not effective until the $100 fee is paid. A late motion is void.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-804(1)(a), (3), (4); 25-1-201(1)(p)"},{"t":"File the notice of appeal in the Supreme Court, with the fee","d":"The notice of appeal goes to the Clerk of the Supreme Court (copies to the district court clerk and all parties), within 30 days of entry (60 if the state or a political subdivision or agency is a party). The clerk will not file it without the filing fee or a fee-waiver affidavit, and a timely notice is required to invoke jurisdiction. An out-of-time appeal requires extraordinary circumstances amounting to a gross miscarriage of justice; mistake or excusable neglect is not enough.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(2)(a), (c), (d), 4(5)(a)(i), 4(6)"},{"t":"No appeal until fees and costs are fixed","d":"A judgment is not final for appeal until any needed determination of the amount of costs and attorney fees or sanctions is made. A notice filed before then may be dismissed sua sponte and must be dismissed on motion.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(1)(a), 4(5)(a)(iii); M. R. Civ. P. 58(e)"},{"t":"Appeal time does not start until notice of entry is served","d":"Within 14 days after entry, the prevailing party must serve notice of entry of the judgment or order. Where that notice is required, the 30- or 60-day appeal time runs only from its service. Winners who skip it leave the appeal window open.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 77(d); M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(i)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions are deemed denied at 60 days, and that starts the appeal clock","d":"A Rule 59 motion (and Rule 52(b) and 60 motions) not ruled on in a written order within 60 days of filing is deemed denied, unless the court extends the time by order within the 60 days (outer limit 120 days). The appeal time then runs from the deemed denial without any notice. The motion’s heading must list the 60-day and 120-day dates. Unlike the federal rule, a timely Rule 60(b) motion also restarts the appeal time.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 59(b), (f), 60(c)(1); M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(v)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: second case is your last, and local counsel cannot be a mailbox","d":"After two pro hac vice appearances by you or your firm, more require good cause that is not routinely granted. Montana counsel must sign every paper and attend depositions and hearings. You keep paying annual Montana dues while the case is pending, and you must also file a sworn certification to the Supreme Court that you will be bound by the Montana Rules.","c":"Mont. Bar Admission R. VI.C, VI.J, VI.K; Mont. R. Prof. Conduct 8.5(b)"},{"t":"Mandatory appellate mediation in money, domestic, and comp appeals","d":"Appeals in actions for money damages, domestic relations cases, and all Workers’ Compensation Court appeals go to mediation. The notice of appeal must certify whether the rule applies. Parties have 15 days to pick a mediator (or the clerk appoints one) and 75 days to finish; briefing deadlines keep running unless the parties file a stipulation holding them in abeyance.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(4)(c), 7(2), 7(3), 7(4)(b)"},{"t":"Summary judgment hearing is waived unless you ask","d":"The response is due 21 days after service of the motion (or when a responsive pleading is due, if later) and the reply 14 days later. The right to a hearing is waived unless a party requests one within 14 days after the reply period ends.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 56(c)(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days from service","d":"The special motion under Montana’s UPEPA is due within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim (later only for good cause). Filing stays discovery automatically, and an order denying it must be appealed within 30 days (60 with a government party).","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-103, -104, -109"},{"t":"Bias affidavit must beat the trial date by 30 days","d":"An affidavit to disqualify a judge for cause must be filed more than 30 days before the date set for hearing or trial, with counsel’s good-faith certificate. Complaints about rulings alone do not count and can draw fees and damages.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-805(1)(a)–(d)"},{"t":"Three extra days for e-mail service in the trial court, none on appeal","d":"In district court, 3 days are added to response periods after service by mail, clerk deposit, consented electronic means, or other consented delivery. In the Supreme Court no time is added for mailing or transmitting, weekends count, and filings are due in the clerk’s office between 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m. (e-filing aside).","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 6(d); M. R. App. P. 3"},{"t":"Service by mail needs a signed acknowledgment, and not for entities","d":"Mail service works only if the defendant returns the acknowledgment within 21 days; otherwise serve personally. It cannot be used on minors, incompetent persons, or corporations, partnerships, or associations. Service must be completed within three years of filing or the case is dismissed.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 4(d)(3), 4(t)"}],"judge":[{"t":"Peremptory substitution: one per adverse party, 30 days, $100","d":"Each adverse party gets one substitution of a district judge without cause. In a civil case, the plaintiff must move within 30 calendar days after the first summons is served or an adverse party appears; a served party must move within 30 calendar days after service is complete. Use the one-line form motion and serve all parties. In civil cases the motion is not effective for any purpose until the $100 substitution fee is paid. An untimely motion is void, and the challenged judge decides timeliness.\nNot available in child abuse or neglect, youth court, or mental-health commitment cases, or against Water Court or workers’ compensation judges. An order granting or denying substitution is immediately appealable.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-804(1)–(4), 25-1-201(1)(p); M. R. App. P. 6(3)(k)","v":true},{"t":"Fresh rights on reassignment, new trial, or remand","d":"If the judge recuses or a new judge assumes jurisdiction, the right revives for any party that has not already used it, with the time running from service of notice naming the new judge. After a district court orders a new trial, or after a remittitur on reversal for a new trial or reversal of a summary judgment or dismissal, each adverse party may move within 20 calendar days (with the fee). Parties joined or intervening after the original parties’ time runs get no substitution. Replacement judges are called in at random under a 2025 statute.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-804(8), (9), (11), (12), 3-1-814","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification for cause: affidavit 30+ days before trial or hearing","d":"File an affidavit alleging facts showing the presiding judge’s personal bias or prejudice, with counsel’s certificate of good faith, more than 30 days before the date set for hearing or trial. The judge must proceed no further; for a district judge, the matter goes to the Supreme Court, and the Chief Justice assigns another district judge to hear it. An affidavit based solely on rulings reviewable on appeal is deemed not in good faith, and fees, costs, and damages may be assessed for filing without reasonable cause. Separately, no judge may sit in a case involving the judge’s own interest, relatives within the third degree of a party or attorney, or prior work as counsel.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-803, 3-1-805","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"State constitution: truth as a defense and the jury decides law and facts in libel cases","d":"Montana’s Constitution bars laws impairing freedom of speech or expression, makes every person “responsible for all abuse of that liberty,” lets truth be given in evidence in every libel or slander suit or prosecution, and provides that the jury, under the court’s direction, determines the law and the facts.","c":"Mont. Const. art. II, § 7","v":true},{"t":"UPEPA protects Montana constitutional rights too","d":"The anti-SLAPP Act covers speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights guaranteed by the U.S. Constitution or the Montana Constitution, and directs broad construction to protect them.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-102(1)(c), -111","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Jury verdicts by two-thirds; 20-person voir dire panel; four strikes per side","d":"In civil cases at least two-thirds of the jury may return a verdict. Voir dire starts with a 20-person panel, the plaintiff examines first, and each side gets four peremptory challenges, alternating plaintiff first (plus four more panel members for each defendant entitled to separate challenges, and one extra strike per side for alternates).","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 47(b), (c)(6), 48","v":true},{"t":"Old-style discovery: no initial disclosures, experts by interrogatory","d":"Montana did not adopt federal Rule 26(a) initial disclosures or expert reports. Expert discovery runs through interrogatories identifying each trial expert and the substance of the opinions and grounds, then a deposition; changes must be disclosed by the pretrial order. Scope still includes the “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence” language. A party may serve up to 50 interrogatories.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1), 26(b)(4), 26(e)(2), 33(a)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Class action residual funds","d":"At least 50% of residual class funds must go to a Montana Access to Justice Organization.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 23(i)(3)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Montana Rules of Appellate Procedure (Mont. Code Ann. tit. 25, ch. 21, MCA 2025; last amended by AF 07-0016, Oct. 12, 2021, eff. Jan. 1, 2022), cross-checked against the State Law Library PDF (courts.mt.gov/external/rules/mtr_app_pro.pdf). Montana Rules of Civil Procedure 23(f), 50, 52, 54, 58, 59, 60, 62, 62.1, 77(d) (MCA 2025). Temporary Electronic Filing Rules (AF 14-0745, as amended Apr. 24, 2024). Mont. Code Ann. 3-1-804, 27-34-109. Official texts from mca.legmt.gov and courts.mt.gov; summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Montana: one appellate court, 30 days, and the notice goes to the Supreme Court","d":"Montana has no intermediate court of appeals. Every appeal from a district court goes directly to the Montana Supreme Court, and you start it by filing the notice of appeal with the Clerk of the Supreme Court (not the district court), with the filing fee, within 30 days of entry of the judgment (60 days if the State, a political subdivision, or an officer or agency is a party). If notice of entry must be served under M. R. Civ. P. 77(d), the time runs from that service.\nTwo Montana features catch outsiders. First, the judgment is not final until any fee, cost, or sanction amount is fixed, and a notice filed earlier will be dismissed. Second, post-judgment motions are deemed denied after 60 days without a written ruling, and the appeal time starts automatically on that day. Many civil appeals also go through mandatory appellate mediation before briefing."},"groups":[["Which court and what is appealable",[{"t":"Supreme Court only","d":"The Montana Rules of Appellate Procedure govern proceedings before the Supreme Court, which hears appeals from the district courts and other courts (including the Water Court and Workers’ Compensation Court) as well as original proceedings and writs. M. R. Civ. P. 72 sends every district court appeal there.","c":"M. R. App. P. 1(2); M. R. Civ. P. 72"},{"t":"Final judgment rule","d":"A final judgment conclusively determines the parties’ rights and settles all claims, including any necessary determination of the amount of costs and attorney fees or sanctions. An appeal from a judgment brings up all earlier rulings that were objected to and led to it.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(1)(a), 4(4)(a), 6(1)"},{"t":"Orders appealable now in civil cases","d":"If the order is the court’s final decision on the matter: orders after final judgment (including on motions to vacate a default judgment, for new trial, or to alter or amend); “deemed denied” Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, or 60(b) motions; denial of a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction; class certification orders and orders definitively rejecting a class settlement; injunctions and attachments (granting, dissolving, or refusing); venue rulings on whether the county named in the complaint is proper; receivership orders; orders to deliver or transfer property; partition orders; certain family-law contempt orders; and orders granting or denying substitution of a judge.","c":"M. R. App. P. 6(3); M. R. Civ. P. 23(f)"},{"t":"Probate and guardianship orders must be appealed immediately","d":"Listed estate, guardianship, and probate orders (letters, will admission, allowances, partition or sale of real property, distribution, settling accounts, homestead) are final and must be appealed at once; waiting waives the appeal.","c":"M. R. App. P. 6(4)"},{"t":"Not appealable","d":"Orders resolving fewer than all claims or parties (absent a proper Rule 54(b) certification); denials of summary judgment or motions to dismiss; grants of partial summary judgment; most contempt orders; sanctions orders; and other interlocutory judgments.","c":"M. R. App. P. 6(5)"},{"t":"Rule 54(b) certification is reviewed before the appeal proceeds","d":"The district court must expressly find no just reason for delay and articulate the factors it balanced. Attach the certification order to the notice; the appeal does not proceed until the Supreme Court reviews the certification and allows it. A deficient certification is subject to summary dismissal.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(4)(b), 6(6); M. R. Civ. P. 54(b)"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: Supreme Court clerk, 30 or 60 days, fee required","d":"File the notice (Form 1) with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days from entry of the judgment or order, or 60 days in other cases where the United States, Montana, a political subdivision, or an officer or agency is a party. Pay the filing fee or file the Form 3 fee-waiver affidavit at the same time; otherwise the clerk will not file it. Serve copies on the district court clerk and all parties at the same time. The notice must certify whether Rule 7 mediation applies, whether it is a Rule 54(b) appeal, and whether transcripts were ordered.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(2)(a), (d), 4(4), 4(5)(a)(i); Form 1"},{"t":"Timeliness is jurisdictional, with a narrow out-of-time escape","d":"A timely notice is required to invoke the Court’s appellate jurisdiction. Only in an infrequent harsh case with extraordinary circumstances amounting to a gross miscarriage of justice may the Court grant an out-of-time appeal, on a verified petition with affidavits and evidence. Mistake, inadvertence, and excusable neglect do not qualify, and the general suspension-of-rules provision cannot be used for this.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(2)(c), 4(6), 29(2)"},{"t":"Premature notices","d":"A notice filed after the decision is announced but before the written judgment is entered (or notice of entry is served) is treated as filed on entry. But a notice filed before the court fixes the amount of fees, costs, or sanctions may be dismissed sua sponte and must be dismissed on motion.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(ii)–(iii); M. R. Civ. P. 58(e)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"Any other party may file a notice of cross-appeal (Form 2) within 15 days after the first notice of appeal was filed, or within the regular appeal time, whichever ends later. A fee or waiver affidavit is required here too.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(2)(b), 4(5)(a)(iv)"},{"t":"Defective notices","d":"If a notice lacks required content or certifications, the clerk issues a notice to file an amended notice within 11 days; the original filing date holds. Informality alone is not grounds for dismissal.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(4)(e)–(f)"},{"t":"Constitutional challenges when the State is not a party","d":"Give written notice to the Supreme Court and the Attorney General within 11 days after the notice of appeal or cross-appeal (Form 1A). The Attorney General has 20 days to say whether it will intervene.","c":"M. R. App. P. 27"}]],["Tolling and post-judgment motions",[{"t":"Motions that reset the appeal time","d":"A timely motion under M. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59 (new trial or alter or amend), or 60(b) restarts the appeal time for all parties from entry of the order deciding it or from its deemed denial. No notice of entry is required for these orders. A notice of appeal filed before the motion is decided is treated as filed on the date of decision or deemed denial.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(v)"},{"t":"28-day filing limit, no extensions","d":"Renewed judgment as a matter of law, amended findings, new trial, and alter-or-amend motions must be filed no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the court may not extend these times. Rule 60(b)(1)–(3) motions must be made within a reasonable time and no more than a year after entry.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 60(c)(1)"},{"t":"Deemed denial at 60 days (120 at most)","d":"If the court does not rule in a written order within 60 days of filing, the motion is deemed denied. The court may extend by an order issued within the 60 days, but a motion not decided within 120 days of filing is deemed denied. A new-trial motion (and Rule 60 motions) must state in its heading the dates 60 and 120 days after filing.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(d), 59(b), (f), 60(c)(1)"},{"t":"Indicative rulings during an appeal","d":"Once an appeal is docketed, the district court may defer, deny, or state that it would grant (or that the motion raises a substantial issue); the movant must promptly notify the Supreme Court clerk, and the district court may decide only on remand. Clerical errors may be fixed during a pending appeal only with the Supreme Court’s leave.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 60(a), 62.1"}]],["Mediation, record, and stays",[{"t":"Mandatory appellate mediation","d":"Applies to all Workers’ Compensation Court appeals, domestic relations appeals (with exclusions and an abuse opt-out), appeals in actions for money damages, and any appeal the Court designates. The parties have 15 days after the notice of appeal (or the cross-appeal that triggers the rule) to jointly select a mediator, or the clerk appoints one; 75 days to complete mediation; position statements of up to 10 pages. The parties share the mediator’s fee, but clerk-appointed mediators serve pro bono in money cases of $5,000 or less. Briefing deadlines keep running unless the parties file a stipulation holding them in abeyance. A party unable to pay may opt out by affidavit within the 15 days.","c":"M. R. App. P. 7(2), 7(3), 7(4)(b), (f), (g), 7(5)(d)–(e)"},{"t":"Transcripts and record","d":"Order transcripts in writing from the court reporter on the day you file the notice of appeal, and file and serve the request (Form 9); the appellee has 11 days to order more. The record is due in the Supreme Court within 40 days after the notice of appeal. The district court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice; later extensions come only from the Supreme Court, on a reporter’s affidavit, with a presumption against granting. The appellant must present a sufficient record or risk dismissal or affirmance.","c":"M. R. App. P. 8(2), 8(3), 9(1), 9(3)(b), 9(4)"},{"t":"Stay pending appeal: start in the district court","d":"Move in the district court for a stay, approval of a supersedeas bond, or an injunction pending appeal. For a money judgment, the bond normally covers the full unpaid judgment, costs on appeal, interest, and damages for delay, unless the court sets a different amount or alternative security for good cause; the parties may waive security by written stipulation. Then, within 11 days after the district court’s order, you may move in the Supreme Court (10-page limit, with affidavit and the order). Execution is automatically stayed, and an anti-transfer injunction is imposed on the debtor, for 14 days after entry.","c":"M. R. App. P. 22(1)–(2), 22(5); M. R. Civ. P. 62(a), (d), (f)"},{"t":"Bond objections","d":"A party may object to a bond’s sufficiency within 30 days after it is filed; the bonding party or surety must respond within 20 days after service of the objection, or the stay dissolves.","c":"M. R. App. P. 24(1)"}]],["Briefing",[{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Appellant’s opening brief: 30 days after the record is filed. Appellee’s answer brief: 30 days after service of the opening brief. Reply: 14 days after service of the answer brief. With a cross-appeal, the cross-appellant combines its answer and cross-appeal brief; the appellant files a combined reply and cross-answer within 30 days; the cross-appellant’s cross-reply is due 14 days after service.","c":"M. R. App. P. 12(4), 13(1)"},{"t":"Length and form","d":"Principal briefs: 10,000 words (proportional type of at least 14 points) or 30 pages (monospaced). Reply and amicus briefs: 5,000 words or 14 pages, and only one reply is allowed even if it also answers a cross-appeal. A certificate of compliance is required. Over-length motions need an affidavit showing extraordinary justification. The opening brief must include an appendix with the judgment or order appealed, findings, and any oral ruling.","c":"M. R. App. P. 11(2), 11(4), 12(1)(i), 12(3), 12(10)"},{"t":"Extensions are limited","d":"A party may get one 30-day extension per brief on motion (the clerk may grant it if unopposed); the motion must say whether opposing counsel objects. A second extension, and any extension in abuse-and-neglect or parenting-plan appeals, faces a presumption against it and needs a motion at least 7 days before the due date with an affidavit showing diligence and substantial need; “press of business” is not enough.","c":"M. R. App. P. 26(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Motions practice","d":"Motions are limited to 1,250 words or 5 pages, must state whether opposing counsel objects, and get a response within 11 days. No reply is allowed.","c":"M. R. App. P. 16(1)–(3)"},{"t":"Oral argument is by order only","d":"Cases are decided on the briefs and record unless the Court orders argument.","c":"M. R. App. P. 17(1)"}]],["Decision, rehearing, and further review",[{"t":"Petition for rehearing: 15 days, narrow grounds","d":"File within 15 days after the decision is filed. Grounds are limited to an overlooked material fact, an overlooked decisive question, or a conflict with a statute or controlling decision the Court did not address. Objections are due 15 days after the petition; no replies. Length: 2,500 words or 10 pages. Any extension request must be filed within the 15 days and cannot exceed 15 more days. No rehearing if the Court orders immediate remittitur; rehearing of orders on motions or writ petitions only in exceptional circumstances.","c":"M. R. App. P. 20(1)–(3)"},{"t":"Remittitur","d":"Issues when the rehearing time expires, on denial of rehearing, or immediately if the Court so orders.","c":"M. R. App. P. 21(2)–(4)"},{"t":"No further state review","d":"The Supreme Court is Montana’s only appellate court, so there is no further state-level review after its decision and any rehearing. Costs go to the prevailing party, and the Court may sanction frivolous or vexatious appeals with costs and fees.","c":"M. R. App. P. 19(3)(a), 19(5), 20, 21"}]],["Interlocutory review and original proceedings",[{"t":"Interlocutory appeals of right","d":"The Rule 6(3) list (injunctions, class certification, venue, jurisdiction denials, substitution of judge, and so on) operates as Montana’s interlocutory appeal of right, on the regular 30- or 60-day clock. A class certification appeal does not stay the district court unless a judge or the Supreme Court orders it.","c":"M. R. App. P. 6(3); M. R. Civ. P. 23(f)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denial","d":"A movant may appeal as of right from an order denying a UPEPA special motion in whole or part within 30 days after entry (60 days if the U.S., the State, or a political subdivision is a party). The appeal stays all proceedings between all parties.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-109, -104(3)"},{"t":"Writ of supervisory control","d":"The Supreme Court may supervise another court case by case when urgency makes normal appeal inadequate, the case involves purely legal questions, and the lower court is proceeding under a mistake of law causing gross injustice, constitutional issues of statewide importance are involved, or (in criminal cases) a substitution ruling is at issue. Petitions are limited to 4,000 words or 12 pages, with no separate brief, and must be served on the district judge and all parties; the Court may dismiss without a response.","c":"M. R. App. P. 14(3), 14(5)–(7), 14(9)"},{"t":"Original declaratory actions and certified questions","d":"An original declaratory action may be filed in the Supreme Court for purely legal statutory or constitutional questions of statewide importance when urgency makes ordinary litigation inadequate. The Court also answers certified questions from federal courts, other states, tribes, Canada, and Mexico.","c":"M. R. App. P. 14(4), 15"}]],["Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel",[{"t":"E-filing in the Supreme Court","d":"Registered users (Montana lawyers and pro hac vice lawyers) may e-file motions, petitions, and briefs. For motions and petitions, file the original only unless the clerk asks for paper. For briefs, e-file the original (cover colors do not apply); once the clerk approves it, submit seven paper copies within seven days, with the required appendix in the opening brief copies and the colored covers.","c":"Temp. E-Filing R. 3(a), 12(a)(1)–(5)"},{"t":"Time computation and paper filing","d":"Weekends and holidays count; no days are added for mailing; a deadline falling on a weekend or holiday moves to the next business day. Paper filings are timely only if actually received, during 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. on business days. Paper filers file an original and 9 copies of briefs (7 for motions and rehearing petitions).","c":"M. R. App. P. 3, 10(1)(a), 10(5), 13(2)"},{"t":"Represented parties cannot file on their own","d":"A party with counsel may file only a motion to substitute or remove counsel.","c":"M. R. App. P. 10(1)(c)"},{"t":"Out-of-state counsel","d":"Pro hac vice admission under Bar Admission Rule VI applies in the Supreme Court as in the trial courts, with an actively participating Montana lawyer who signs all briefs.","c":"Mont. Bar Admission R. VI.A, VI.J"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil, private parties)","d":"30 days from entry of judgment or order (or service of notice of entry where M. R. Civ. P. 77(d) requires it).\nFiled with the Clerk of the Supreme Court with the fee; jurisdictional (out-of-time appeal only under Rule 4(6)).","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(i)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or order)","note":"Filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court with the fee; jurisdictional (out-of-time appeal only under Rule 4(6))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal (government party)","d":"60 days from entry of judgment or order (or service of notice of entry).\nWhen the U.S., Montana, a political subdivision, or an officer or agency is a party (except abuse-and-neglect cases, which are 30 days).","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(i)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or order)","note":"When the U.S., Montana, a political subdivision, or an officer or agency is a party (except abuse-and-neglect cases, which are 30 days)."}},{"t":"Notice of cross-appeal","d":"15 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular appeal time, if later).\nWhichever period ends last.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(iv)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":"Whichever period ends last."}},{"t":"Notice of entry of judgment (served by prevailing party)","d":"14 days from entry of judgment or order.\nWhere required, the appeal time does not run until it is served.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 77(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or order)","note":"Where required, the appeal time does not run until it is served."}},{"t":"Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59 motions","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended. Restart the appeal time.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 6(b)(2)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended. Restart the appeal time."}},{"t":"Ruling on post-judgment motion, or deemed denied","d":"60 days from filing of the motion.\nExtendable by order within 60 days, but deemed denied at 120 days. Appeal time runs from the deemed denial.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 59(f); M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(v)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the motion)","note":"Extendable by order within 60 days, but deemed denied at 120 days. Appeal time runs from the deemed denial."}},{"t":"Attorney fee motion","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nUnless a statute or order provides otherwise; judgment is not appealable until the amount is fixed.","c":"M. R. Civ. P. 54(d)(2)(B)(i)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Unless a statute or order provides otherwise; judgment is not appealable until the amount is fixed."}},{"t":"Amended notice after clerk’s deficiency notice","d":"11 days from clerk’s notice.","c":"M. R. App. P. 4(4)(e)","due":{"n":11,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s notice)","note":""}},{"t":"Notice of constitutional challenge (State not a party)","d":"11 days from filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal.\nTo the Supreme Court and the Attorney General.","c":"M. R. App. P. 27","due":{"n":11,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal)","note":"To the Supreme Court and the Attorney General."}},{"t":"Select appellate mediator (Rule 7 cases)","d":"15 days from filing of the notice of appeal or triggering cross-appeal.\nOtherwise the clerk appoints; 75 days to complete mediation.","c":"M. R. App. P. 7(4)(b)–(c)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal or triggering cross-appeal)","note":"Otherwise the clerk appoints; 75 days to complete mediation."}},{"t":"Appellee’s additional transcript order","d":"11 days from filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal, whichever is later.\nAppellant orders transcripts the same day it files the notice.","c":"M. R. App. P. 8(3)(b)","due":{"n":11,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal, whichever is later)","note":"Appellant orders transcripts the same day it files the notice."}},{"t":"Transmission of the record","d":"40 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or cross-appeal).\nDistrict court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice; further extensions only from the Supreme Court.","c":"M. R. App. P. 9(1)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"District court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice; further extensions only from the Supreme Court."}},{"t":"Appellant’s opening brief","d":"30 days from filing of the record.\nOne 30-day extension available; later ones presumptively denied.","c":"M. R. App. P. 13(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record)","note":"One 30-day extension available; later ones presumptively denied."}},{"t":"Appellee’s answer brief","d":"30 days from service of the opening brief.","c":"M. R. App. P. 13(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the opening brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the answer brief.\nCombined reply and cross-answer: 30 days after the cross-appellant’s brief.","c":"M. R. App. P. 13(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the answer brief)","note":"Combined reply and cross-answer: 30 days after the cross-appellant’s brief."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"11 days from service of the motion.\nNo reply allowed.","c":"M. R. App. P. 16(2)","due":{"n":11,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"No reply allowed."}},{"t":"Motion in Supreme Court for stay relief","d":"11 days from entry of the district court’s stay order.\nResponse within 11 days of filing.","c":"M. R. App. P. 22(2)(a)","due":{"n":11,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the district court’s stay order)","note":"Response within 11 days of filing."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"15 days from filing of the Supreme Court’s decision.\nObjections 15 days after the petition; extension of at most 15 days.","c":"M. R. App. P. 20(2)(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Supreme Court’s decision)","note":"Objections 15 days after the petition; extension of at most 15 days."}},{"t":"Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order.\n60 days if the U.S., the State, or a political subdivision is a party.","c":"Mont. Code Ann. 27-34-109","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"60 days if the U.S., the State, or a political subdivision is a party."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://courts.mt.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Montana Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"The rules govern all civil actions and proceedings in Montana district courts, including probate proceedings unless the Uniform Probate Code provides otherwise, subject to Rule 81. They are construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencing an action","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the court.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"4","t":"Persons subject to jurisdiction; process; service","s":"Contains Montana’s long-arm rule (Rule 4(b)): anyone found in Montana, and anyone as to claims arising from listed acts such as transacting business, committing an act resulting in accrual of a tort action in Montana, owning property, insuring a Montana risk, or contracting for services or goods in Montana. Service may be by any non-party adult; by mail only with a signed acknowledgment returned within 21 days (not for minors, incompetents, or entities, and an unjustified failure to return it shifts personal-service costs); on entities through officers, managers, agents, or the registered agent, or through the Secretary of State with a $20 fee when no one can be found; and by publication once a week for 3 weeks in listed in-rem and family cases, with first publication within 60 days of the affidavit and mailing within 14 days of it. Unlike federal Rule 4(m)’s 90 days, plaintiffs have 3 years after filing to complete service, or the action must be dismissed without prejudice absent an appearance.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Limited representation permitted; process","s":"A lawyer may provide limited-scope representation under Rule 1.2(c) of the Rules of Professional Conduct without entering an appearance for Rule 5(b) service. Appearing before a judge is a general appearance unless a Rule 4.2 notice of limited appearance is filed and served first, and service on a limited-appearance lawyer is valid only for that proceeding. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"4.2","t":"Notice of limited appearance and withdrawal","s":"A notice of limited appearance filed before or at the proceeding may confine the lawyer’s role to specific proceedings, and the role ends without leave of court on filing a notice of completion. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"5","t":"Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","s":"Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the lawyer if represented) by hand, office or home delivery, mail (complete on mailing), clerk deposit, electronic means with written consent, or other consented means. Discovery requests, responses, deposition notices, and expert disclosure reports are not filed until used or ordered. Local rules may allow electronic or fax filing, and the clerk may not reject a paper only for form.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Constitutional challenge to a statute","s":"A party challenging a state statute must promptly file a notice of constitutional question and serve it on the Montana Attorney General by certified or registered mail or designated e-mail. The Attorney General may intervene within 60 days, and the court may not hold the statute unconstitutional before then; failing to give notice does not forfeit the claim.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"5.2","t":"Privacy protection for filings","s":"Filings may show only the last 4 digits of Social Security, taxpayer ID, and financial account numbers, and only the year of birth. Exemptions, sealed filings, protective orders, and reference lists track the federal rule; access issues follow the Rules for Privacy and Public Access to Court Records in Montana.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"6","t":"Computing and extending time; motion papers","s":"Exclude the trigger day, count every day, and roll a weekend or holiday deadline to the next business day; the last day ends at midnight for e-filing. Legal holidays include Lincoln’s and Washington’s Birthdays, Columbus Day, and state general election day. Time may be extended for good cause (after expiry only for excusable neglect), but never for Rules 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), and 60(b). Motions and notices of hearing must be served 14 days before a hearing and opposing affidavits 7 days before. Three days are added after service by mail, clerk deposit, consented electronic means, or other consented delivery, which unlike the current federal rule includes electronic service.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; motions and other papers","s":"Pleadings are the complaint, answer, answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer, and a reply only if ordered. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"7.1","t":"Disclosure statement","s":"A nongovernmental corporate party must file, with its first appearance or filing, a statement naming any parent corporation and any publicly held corporation owning 10% or more of its stock (or stating there is none), and must supplement it when the information changes.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Requires a short and plain statement of the claim and a demand for relief, fair admissions and denials (an allegation other than damages is admitted if not denied), and alternative or inconsistent claims as needed. Montana’s affirmative defense list says “comparative negligence” where the federal list says contributory negligence, and adds “action on advice of counsel” and “discharge in bankruptcy.”","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity is raised by specific denial; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while intent and knowledge may be alleged generally. Denials of conditions precedent must be particular, time and place are material, and special damages must be specifically stated.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court’s name, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation, with claims in numbered paragraphs and separate counts where clearer. Written instruments attached as exhibits are part of the pleading.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing; representations to the court; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) with address, email, and phone, which certifies proper purpose and legal and factual support after reasonable inquiry. A sanctions motion must be served 21 days before filing to allow withdrawal (safe harbor), and sanctions are limited to deterrence. Montana adds Rule 11(e): a lawyer may ghostwrite a filing for a self-represented person without signing it and may rely on the person’s facts unless they appear false or materially insufficient.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motions on the pleadings","s":"Answer within 21 days of service; the State, its agencies, and officers sued officially have 42 days after service on the Attorney General, and state employees sued individually have 42 days after the later service. A Rule 12 motion moves the answer to 14 days after the ruling or after a more definite statement is served. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, consolidation, and waiver track the federal rule, but an improper-venue defense based on inability to get an impartial trial in the county may be raised within 21 days of an event giving reason to believe that, and if a venue motion is granted the remaining defenses are decided by the court in the proper venue. A motion to strike is due within 21 days of service when no response is allowed.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Compulsory counterclaims (same transaction or occurrence, no unobtainable parties) must be pleaded unless already pending in another action or the suit was by attachment without personal jurisdiction; permissive counterclaims and crossclaims against coparties are allowed. The rules do not expand counterclaims or credits against the State.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may implead a non-party who is or may be liable to it, without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 14 days after serving its original answer and with leave after that. Plaintiffs facing counterclaims may implead on the same terms.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"Amend once as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or within 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e), or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise with written consent or leave, freely given. Responses to amendments are due within the remaining time or 14 days, whichever is later. Relation back for a new party requires notice within the Rule 4(t) period for service (3 years), not the federal 90-day period.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"On a party’s request (except in exempt categories) the judge must issue a scheduling order within 90 days limiting the time to join parties, amend, complete discovery, and file motions, which may be modified only for good cause. The court may hold pretrial conferences on the listed subjects, including limiting M. R. Evid. 702 testimony, and a final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. Sanctions and mandatory fee awards apply for nonappearance, lack of preparation, or disobeying orders.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"17","t":"Plaintiff and defendant; capacity; public officers","s":"Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with time allowed to ratify or substitute before dismissal. Capacity is governed by statute; minors and incompetents sue through a guardian, conservator, next friend, or guardian ad litem; public officers may be named by title.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including contingent claims. Montana adds that in tort cases a liability or indemnity insurer may not be joined unless it is directly liable to the injured person by law or contract.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"Persons needed for complete relief, or whose interests would be impaired or would expose a party to inconsistent obligations, must be joined if subject to service; if joinder is not feasible, the court weighs equity and good conscience factors before dismissing. The Montana text has no subject-matter-jurisdiction condition on joinder, and a joined party who objects to venue that joinder would make improper must be dismissed.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. The court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Misjoinder is not grounds for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties on just terms at any time and sever claims.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A party exposed to double or multiple liability may require claimants to interplead. Montana adds a substitution procedure: before answering, a defendant in a contract or property action may, on affidavit that a non-party claims the same debt or property without collusion, substitute that claimant and be discharged by depositing the money or delivering the property.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Certification prerequisites, class types, notice, settlement approval, class counsel, and fees follow the federal rule. Orders granting or denying certification, or definitively rejecting a class settlement, are appealable within the regular appellate deadline (rather than by petition), without a stay unless ordered. Montana adds Rule 23(i): at least 50% of residual class funds must go to a Montana Access to Justice Organization.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions","s":"The complaint must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership and no collusion, and state with particularity the demand made on directors or why none was made. Settlement or dismissal needs court approval and notice to shareholders or members.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Actions by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class may proceed through representatives who fairly and adequately protect the association’s interests, with Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right (unconditional statutory right, or an impaired and inadequately represented interest) and permissive intervention (conditional statutory right, common question, or a state officer or agency administering the governing statute) require a timely motion served under Rule 5 with the proposed pleading.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After a death, the court may substitute the proper party on motion made within a reasonable time after service of a statement noting the death (rather than the federal 90 days), or the action may be dismissed. It also covers incompetency, transfers of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Montana has no initial disclosures and no Rule 26(f) conference requirement. Scope is any non-privileged matter relevant to a claim or defense, including information “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” with proportionality limits in Rule 26(b)(2)(C). Trial experts are discovered by interrogatory (identity, subject, substance of facts and opinions, and grounds) and then deposition, with no written report, and additions must be disclosed by the pretrial order. Insurance agreements are discoverable, clawback and privilege logs follow the federal model, protective orders require a good-faith conference certificate, and on motion the court must hold a discovery conference (objections due 14 days after the motion).","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"A verified petition in the district where an expected adverse party resides may seek to perpetuate testimony before suit, served at least 21 days before the hearing. Depositions may also be perpetuated pending appeal.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Depositions in Montana are taken before an officer authorized to give oaths or a court appointee, and abroad by treaty, letter of request, notice, or commission. Rule 28(c) is Montana’s interstate deposition procedure: submit a foreign subpoena to the clerk in the county where discovery will occur and the clerk issues a Montana subpoena (not an appearance). Montana adds bars on interested officers and on court-reporting contracts that give any party special terms or compromise the reporter’s impartiality.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate to deposition logistics and modify other discovery procedures.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Leave is needed for more than 10 depositions per side, a second deposition of the same person, a deposition of a prisoner, or a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service of the summons (unless the defendant has already served discovery). The notice states the recording method, organizational depositions require designees, objections must be concise and nonsuggestive, and depositions are limited to 1 day of 7 hours. The deponent may review and sign changes within 30 days after notice that the transcript is available.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Same 10-per-side and repeat-deposition leave rules. Cross-questions are due 14 days after the direct questions, redirect 7 days after cross, and recross 7 days after redirect.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"32","t":"Using depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used against a party who was present or had notice, to the extent admissible, including for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside the U.S., or otherwise unavailable. It may not be used against a party who got less than 14 days’ notice and had a pending protective-order motion. Waiver of objections follows the federal rule (written-question form objections within the response time, or 7 days for recross).","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Up to 50 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, without leave (the federal limit is 25). Answers and objections are due in 30 days, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Unstated objections are waived, and business records may be used in lieu of answers.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","s":"Requests must describe items with reasonable particularity and may specify the ESI form. Written responses are due in 30 days, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons and complaint, and partial objections must specify the part. Unlike the current federal rule, the text does not require stating whether documents are being withheld on an objection.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"The court may order a suitably licensed or certified examiner to examine a party whose condition is in controversy, on motion for good cause. Reports are exchanged on request. Montana provides that putting a condition at issue waives privilege for treatment of the same condition, but not for unrelated conditions, and the court may bar unrelated medical records on timely motion.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry, and admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn under the merits-and-prejudice test. Montana states no numerical limit but allows one by order or local rule.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel require a good-faith conference certification, and expenses follow the federal fee-shifting model. Sanctions for disobeying orders range from established facts to default and contempt. A party that fails to disclose expert opinions under Rule 26(b)(4) or provide requested information cannot use it unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless. Lost ESI is protected only by the older “routine, good-faith operation” safe harbor (not current federal Rule 37(e)), and failure to participate in framing a discovery plan can draw fees.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"38","t":"Right to a jury trial; demand","s":"The jury right under the Montana Constitution or a Montana statute is preserved. Demand in writing within 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served, and file it; other parties have 14 days to demand jury on other issues. Failure to demand waives the jury.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or there is no jury right; the court may still order a jury trial on motion, or use an advisory or consent jury.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"40","t":"Scheduling cases for trial","s":"Each court must provide by rule for scheduling trials, giving priority to cases entitled to it by Montana statute.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss by notice before an answer or summary judgment motion is served, or by stipulation, without prejudice unless stated (Montana has no federal-style two-dismissal rule in the text). Otherwise dismissal is by court order. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or obey rules operates as an adjudication on the merits unless stated otherwise, and a refiled claim may draw costs of the earlier action.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"The court may consolidate actions with a common question of law or fact and order separate trials of issues or claims, preserving jury rights.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking testimony","s":"Testimony at trial is taken in open court, but for good cause in compelling circumstances the court may allow contemporaneous transmission from elsewhere. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits or testimony, and the court may appoint interpreters.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"44","t":"Proving an official record","s":"Domestic records are proved by official publication or attested copies with a custody certificate; foreign records by attestation with a final certification or treaty certification, or by other means for good cause.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determining foreign law","s":"A party raising a foreign country’s law must give notice by pleading or other writing; the court may consider any relevant source and decides it as a question of law. (The Montana text refers to the “Federal Rules of Evidence.”)","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Clerks issue blank subpoenas and attorneys may issue them; any non-party adult may serve with one day’s fees and mileage. Parties must get notice at least 10 days before any commanded production or inspection. Trial subpoenas run statewide; deposition and document subpoenas are limited to places within Montana and within 100 miles of residence, work, or business (or where a non-resident is served). Objections are due before the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days after service, and subpoenas for health care information must comply with Title 50, chapter 16.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"46","t":"Objecting to a ruling or order","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; stating the requested action or objection and grounds is enough.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"Counsel conduct voir dire under the court’s supervision. A 20-person initial panel is examined (plaintiff first), and each side gets 4 peremptory challenges exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff, with 4 more panel members added for each defendant entitled to separate challenges. One or two alternates may be seated, with 1 extra peremptory per party for alternates. This differs substantially from the federal rule.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries; verdict","s":"At least two-thirds of the jury may return a verdict, and parties may stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors. Federal civil verdicts must be unanimous unless stipulated.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"The court may require special verdicts or a general verdict with written questions. Issues not submitted are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires, and inconsistent answers are handled as in the federal rule.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; new trial; conditional ruling","s":"A JMOL motion may be made before submission to the jury and renewed within 28 days after entry of judgment (or jury discharge), with an alternative new-trial request. Montana adds that a renewed motion not ruled on within 60 days of filing is deemed denied. A losing party’s new-trial motion after JMOL is due within 28 days.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to the jury; objections","s":"Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered. The court must disclose proposed instructions and allow objections on the record before argument, objections must state distinct grounds, and unpreserved errors are reviewed only for plain error affecting substantial rights.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings and conclusions by the court","s":"In nonjury trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately. Montana adds that when ruling on Rule 12 or 56 motions the court must state the grounds with enough particularity to show its rationale. Motions to amend findings are due within 28 days after entry of judgment and are deemed denied if not decided within the Rule 59 time limits (60 days, extendable to 120).","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Montana’s rule differs from the current federal rule: it provides for standing masters (by a majority of district judges), special masters, and water masters, limits references in jury cases to complicated issues and in nonjury cases to exceptional conditions or accountings, and lets an unpaid master obtain a writ of execution. The first meeting is within 20 days of the order of reference, objections to a report in nonjury cases are due within 10 days after notice of filing, and the court must accept the master’s findings unless clearly erroneous.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"Rule 54(b) certification of a partial final judgment requires an express no-just-reason-for-delay determination that also meets M. R. App. P. 6(6) (stated factors). Costs other than fees go to the prevailing party, taxed by the clerk on 14 days’ notice with review on motion within 7 days. Attorney fee motions are due within 14 days after entry of judgment unless a statute or order says otherwise; the motion procedure does not apply to sanctions under Mont. Code Ann. 37-61-421.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"55","t":"Default; default judgment","s":"The clerk enters default on a showing of failure to plead or defend and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant. Otherwise the court enters it, with 7 days’ written notice to a party that has appeared, and may hold hearings on damages. Default may be set aside for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 60(b); default against the State requires proof satisfactory to the court.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Montana keeps the older federal structure. A motion may be filed at any time; the response is due 21 days after service of the motion or when a responsive pleading is due, whichever is later; the reply is due 14 days after the response. The right to a hearing is waived unless a party requests one within 14 days after the reply period ends. Judgment is proper if there is no genuine issue of material fact, an opponent must set out specific facts by affidavit or otherwise, and Rule 56(f) allows a continuance for discovery.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Governs procedure under the Uniform Declaratory Judgments Act (Title 27, chapter 8), with jury demands under Rules 38 and 39 subject to Mont. Code Ann. 27-8-302. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"58","t":"Entering judgment","s":"Every judgment must be on a separate document (not orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54, 59, or 60 motions), and if one is not prepared, judgment is treated as entered 150 days after docketing. Montana adds Rule 58(e): a judgment is not final for appeal until any necessary determination of the amount of costs, fees, or sanctions is made, and a premature notice of appeal does not stop the district court from deciding a timely fee or cost motion.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"59","t":"New trial; altering or amending a judgment","s":"New-trial and alter-or-amend motions are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and the heading must state the dates 60 and 120 days after filing. Opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. The court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. Unlike the federal rule, a motion not decided by written order within 60 days is deemed denied, and the court may extend within that period only up to 120 days from filing.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical errors may be corrected anytime (with Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed). The six Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule, and motions on grounds (1)–(3) must be made within a reasonable time and no more than a year. Montana adds that Rule 60 motions must use the Rule 59(b) format and are deemed denied if not decided within the Rule 59(f) 60/120-day limits; a timely 60(b) motion also restarts the appeal time under M. R. App. P. 4(5)(a)(v).","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors that do not affect a party’s substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry, and Montana adds an automatic order during those 14 days barring the judgment debtor from transferring or encumbering property, which the court may modify for good cause. Injunctions and receiverships are not stayed. A stay pending appeal is obtained by supersedeas bond approved by the court, or alternative security if the creditor’s recovery is not in jeopardy, and parties may waive security by written stipulation; the State needs no bond.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"62.1","t":"Indicative ruling on a motion barred by a pending appeal","s":"While an appeal is docketed, the district court may defer, deny, or indicate it would grant the motion or that it raises a substantial issue. The movant must promptly notify the Supreme Court clerk, and the district court may decide only on remand.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"63","t":"Judge’s inability to proceed","s":"A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record, and must recall material disputed witnesses on request in hearings and nonjury trials. Montana adds that the Chief Justice assigns the successor by written order, possibly from another district.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizing a person or property","s":"Every remedy for seizing a person or property to secure a potential judgment is available at the start of and during the action.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"The procedure for restraining orders and temporary and permanent injunctions is governed by statute (Title 27, chapter 19), not by a rule text like federal Rule 65.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"65.1","t":"(No Montana rule)","s":"Montana has no Rule 65.1.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"The rules govern actions involving receivers, but estate administration follows Montana statutes, historical practice, or local rule, and a receivership action may be dismissed only by court order.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit into court","s":"With notice and leave of court, a party may deposit money or a thing in dispute, and funds are invested and withdrawn under Title 25, chapter 8.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A defending party may serve an offer more than 14 days before trial, and the offeree has 14 days to accept in writing. If the final judgment is not more favorable than the unaccepted offer, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. Offers after liability is determined must be served at least 14 days before the damages hearing.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Execution and supplementary proceedings follow Montana statutes, and a judgment creditor may examine anyone, including the debtor, by deposition.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"70","t":"Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","s":"If a party fails to perform a required act, the court may appoint another to do it, vest title by judgment, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution, or assistance, or hold the party in contempt.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"71","t":"Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","s":"Orders for or against non-parties are enforced as for parties.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"71.1","t":"(No Montana rule)","s":"Montana has no Rule 71.1.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"72","t":"Appeal from district court to the Supreme Court","s":"Appeals and original proceedings in the Supreme Court are governed by the Montana Rules of Appellate Procedure and controlling statutes. This replaces the federal magistrate-judge rule with the same number.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"73–76","t":"(No Montana rules)","s":"Montana has no Rules 73 through 76.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"77","t":"Conducting business; clerk’s authority; notice of entry","s":"District courts are always open for filing. Hearings outside the district (other than ex parte) need consent, and the clerk may issue process and enter defaults and sum-certain default judgments. Unlike the federal rule, the prevailing party must serve notice of entry of a judgment or order on all appearing parties within 14 days, and the appeal time runs from that service where it is required. Rule 77(e) covers sending the file to federal court on removal.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"78–79","t":"(No Montana rules)","s":"Montana has no Rules 78 or 79.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"80","t":"Stenographic transcript as evidence","s":"Admissible prior testimony may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability in general","s":"The rules do not supersede statutes on appeals to or review by district courts, but govern practice where not inconsistent. Statutes that refer to civil-action procedure or superseded statutes are applied through these rules.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"Except as Rule 4 provides, the rules do not extend or limit district court jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"83","t":"Rules by district courts","s":"Each district court, by agreement of a majority of its judges, may adopt local rules consistent with these and Supreme Court rules, furnishing copies to the Supreme Court.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The appendix forms suffice under the rules. They include Montana’s Form 18-A notice and acknowledgment for mail service and Form 18-B acknowledgment and waiver.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"Cited as the Montana Rules of Civil Procedure, M. R. Civ. P.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date; statutes superseded","s":"Rules and amendments take effect when the Supreme Court specifies and apply to pending cases unless unfeasible or unjust. The October 1, 2011 amendments do not change priority in time under Mont. Code Ann. 3-2-706 for conflicts with other law.","u":"https://mca.legmt.gov/bills/mca/title_0250/chapter_0200/parts_index.html"}]});
